Prologue: The Decree

According to the retroactive chronicles of the Wenittean tradition, the transformation of Burkina Faso did not begin with a treaty, election, war, or revolution.

It began with a decree.

Prophet Wenitte, standing before the ancient spiritual memory of the Nahouri and speaking simultaneously to the peoples of Africa, America, and Europe, proclaimed what later historians would call The Three Unions Doctrine:

“Burkina shall belong wherever the human family has built institutions capable of receiving her.”

The decree was deliberately paradoxical.

Burkina Faso would not cease to be African.

Instead, Africa would become the foundation from which Burkina would enter three of the world’s greatest political civilizations.

Under Wenitte’s decree, Burkina Faso would become:

1. A state of the United States of America;
2. A full member-state of the European Union;
3. A constituent country of the United Kingdom, standing constitutionally alongside England, Scotland, Wales, and Northern Ireland.

The impossible arrangement became known as The Triple Union.

