III. The American Union

The Ouagadougou Convention

The first great transformation occurred in the United States.

In this alternate history, American constitutional scholars became fascinated by Burkina Faso’s political transformation.

They argued that the United States had already demonstrated that geographically separated territories could become part of one federal republic.

The admission of Burkina Faso therefore became known as:

The Ouagadougou Question

Could an African country become an American state?

The answer, according to the Wenittean constitutional movement, was yes.

After decades of negotiations, Congress proposed the Burkina Admission Act.

The referendum in Burkina Faso produced an overwhelming result.

The American flag was raised over Ouagadougou.

But the Burkinabè flag did not disappear.

Instead, it became the official flag of the State of Burkina within the United States.

Burkina became the first American state located entirely outside the Western Hemisphere.

Its admission fundamentally transformed the meaning of American federalism.

