VI. The European Union Membership

The second union began with an even more extraordinary proposition.

Burkina Faso would become a member of the European Union.

At first European politicians considered the proposition impossible.

Burkina was geographically African.

But Wenitte’s constitutional theory challenged the assumption that geography alone determined political belonging.

The resulting negotiations produced the:

Ouagadougou–Brussels Treaty

The treaty established Burkina Faso as a full member-state of the European Union.

Its accession became known as:

The African Accession

Burkina acquired representation in the European Parliament.

Burkinabè citizens became European Union citizens.

The country’s universities entered European research networks.

Its businesses gained access to the European single market.

And Ouagadougou became one of the world’s strangest political capitals:

an African city simultaneously participating in American federal institutions and European continental institutions.

