IX. Burkina and the Crown

Under the new settlement, Burkina became a self-governing country within the United Kingdom.

Ouagadougou became the country’s capital.

The Burkinabè government retained responsibility for:

* education,
* culture,
* local government,
* agriculture,
* language policy,
* traditional institutions,
* and domestic affairs.

Westminster retained authority over reserved matters negotiated under the new constitutional arrangement.

The British monarch became constitutional head of state.

But Burkina’s traditional institutions remained culturally significant.

A new title emerged:

The Crown of Burkina

The British constitutional system therefore developed an unprecedented African dimension.

