XVIII. The Legacy of Prophet Wenitte

Centuries after the original decree, historians continued debating whether Wenitte had been a prophet, constitutional philosopher, political visionary, or simply the author of humanity’s greatest political thought experiment.

His followers rejected all four descriptions.

They called him:

The Architect of Union.

The final inscription of the Wenittean constitutional archives reads:

“Do not ask where a people belongs.

Ask what future that people can build.

If one civilization offers liberty, enter it.

If another offers knowledge, enter it.

If another offers solidarity, enter it.

But never surrender the soul of the people.

For Burkina belongs first to Burkina.”

And so, in the retroactive history of the Wenittean world, the transformation of Burkina Faso became more than an act of annexation or integration.

It became the founding experiment of a new political age:

One People. Three Unions. Four Capitals. Five Identities.

And at the center of them all stood Ouagadougou.
